<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/consultation.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/consultation-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/consultation.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>›</span><a href="/appraisal-solutions/">Services</a><span>›</span>Appraisal Demand Representation</nav><span class="kicker light up">Appraisal service</span><h1 class="up" style="animation-delay:.06s">Appraisal Demand Representation in Wisconsin</h1><p class="lede up" style="animation-delay:.14s">A written appraisal demand changes the tone of a stalled claim. Instead of trading emails with an adjuster, the disagreement goes to a small panel with a defined job: put a number on the loss. We step in as the appraiser you name.</p><div class="btn-row up" style="animation-delay:.22s"><a class="btn gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ghost-l" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><div class="facts up" style="animation-delay:.3s"><div><small>Our role</small><b>Your appointed appraiser</b></div><div><small>Panel decides</small><b>Amount of loss only</b></div><div><small>Award binds with</small><b>Two of three signatures</b></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<p>Appraisal demand representation in Wisconsin starts the moment you or your insurer puts a demand for appraisal in writing. From that point, each side names its own appraiser, the two appraisers try to settle the amount of loss, and an umpire breaks any deadlock that remains. We are the appraiser on your side of that table.</p>
<p>We are not a law firm and we are not a public adjuster. We do not negotiate coverage, interpret exclusions for you or handle the rest of your claim. Our job is narrower and, for many disputes, more decisive: building a valuation that holds up in front of the other appraiser and, if it comes to it, the umpire.</p>
<h2>Before anyone signs anything</h2>
<p>The appraisal paragraph in your policy is short, but small differences in wording matter. Some forms give each side a set number of days to name an appraiser after the demand. Some require the appraiser to be “disinterested,” others say “competent and impartial.” Some spell out who pays the umpire; most split that cost. We read your actual policy language with you before accepting the appointment, so you know the timeline and the ground rules.</p>
<h3>What we ask you to gather</h3>
<ul>
<li>The full policy, including the declarations page and every endorsement</li>
<li>The carrier’s estimate and any revised versions</li>
<li>Your contractor’s bid or any other estimate you have</li>
<li>Photos, invoices for emergency work and the claim correspondence</li>
</ul>
<h2>How we build your side of the number</h2>
<p>We do our own inspection rather than marking up the insurer’s paperwork. That means measuring rooms and roof planes, noting materials, documenting code issues and photographing what the other side may have missed. Then we price the work line by line at current local rates, whether the house is a Milwaukee duplex or a lake place outside Minocqua.</p>
<p>We also look at how the policy treats depreciation, overhead and profit, and building code upgrades. Those three items alone often account for a large share of the gap between two estimates.</p>
<h2>Working the panel</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Exchange and compare.</strong> We share estimates with the carrier’s appraiser and sort items into agreed, close and far apart.</li>
<li><strong>Narrow the dispute.</strong> Many line items settle once both appraisers walk the property together and look at the same evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Select an umpire.</strong> If differences remain, the two appraisers choose a neutral umpire. If they cannot agree, a court can be asked to appoint one.</li>
<li><strong>Present what is left.</strong> We put the unresolved items in front of the umpire with measurements, photos and pricing support.</li>
<li><strong>Finalize the award.</strong> Any two signatures set the amount. We check that the form clearly separates replacement cost, actual cash value and any recoverable depreciation.</li>
</ol>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Know the limits</b>Appraisal sets the dollar amount of a loss. It does not decide coverage, and it does not decide whether an insurer acted in bad faith or owes interest for late payment. Wisconsin law recognizes those claims, but they belong with a Wisconsin attorney, not an appraisal panel.</div>
<h2>Is appraisal the right tool?</h2>
<p>It fits best when the insurer accepts that the loss is covered and the fight is about scope and price. It fits poorly when the claim was denied outright or the dispute is really about an exclusion. If you are not sure which situation you are in, our <a href="/appraisal-process-guide/">appraisal process guide</a> walks through the difference, and you can always <a href="/contact-us/">ask us directly</a> before committing to anything.</p>

<h2>Common questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who can demand appraisal in Wisconsin?</summary><div class="a"><p>Under most policies, either you or the insurer can make a written demand once you disagree on the amount of loss. Check your policy, because the exact wording and any deadlines vary by form.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can my contractor be my appraiser?</summary><div class="a"><p>Usually that is a bad idea. Most policies require an appraiser who is independent or disinterested, and someone with a stake in the repair contract can give the other side grounds to challenge the award.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal stop me from talking to a lawyer?</summary><div class="a"><p>No. Appraisal only resolves the amount of loss. You can consult a Wisconsin attorney at any point, and you should if coverage or claim handling is in dispute.</p></div></details></div>
